 <section id="port-config" class="mb-16">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">3. Car Charger Port Configuration: USB-C, USB-A &amp; Multi-Port</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">Car charger port configuration directly impacts SKU count, return rates, and customer satisfaction for B2B buyers. For new OEM inventory, <strong>prioritize USB-C PD ports as the primary interface while maintaining a single USB-A port for legacy compatibility</strong>, covering 95%+ of user scenarios without increasing SKU complexity. Dual-port USB-C + USB-A configurations reduce procurement complexity by 50% versus managing separate single-port SKUs for each connector type.</p>
 <h3 class="font-black text-slate-900 uppercase mb-4">USB-C vs USB-A: The Regulatory Shift</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">USB-A caps at ~12W (5V/2.4A); USB-C with PD delivers 100W+ from the same form factor. <strong>The EU Common Charger Directive (EU 2022/2380)</strong> mandates USB-C for all smartphones, tablets, and cameras from December 2024, laptops by April 2026. For B2B car charger buyers targeting the European market, USB-C PD is now a regulatory requirement, not a differentiator.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm mb-4">As of 2026, 89% of new smartphones ship with USB-C globally (Counterpoint Research). USB-A-only car chargers are becoming obsolete for forward-looking procurement, maintaining a single USB-A port covers the remaining 11% without adding a second SKU.</p>
 <h3 class="font-black text-slate-900 uppercase mb-4">Single vs Dual Port Decision</h3>
