 <!-- FAQ -->
 <section id="faq" class="mb-16">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-8 text-center">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
 <div class="space-y-6 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What wattage car charger should procurement managers specify for different fleet types?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer"><strong>Single-device</strong> (sales rep, personal vehicle): 20-30W PD single-port. <strong>Mixed fleet with laptop users:</strong> 65W dual-port PD 3.1 GaN covers phone + laptop simultaneously, reducing SKU complexity. <strong>Multi-passenger</strong> (ride-share, family): 4+ port hub with 60W total. <strong>Commercial fleet with branding:</strong> 65W GaN dual-port with custom logo and E-Mark certification. <strong>24V commercial vehicles</strong> (trucks, buses): specify wide-input 12-24V compatibility, the $0.50-1.00 BOM adder for wide-input regulation eliminates voltage-mismatch returns entirely. FOB Shenzhen at 1,000 units: $2-4 for 20W to $8-14 for 65W GaN fleet-grade.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">GaN vs silicon car chargers, real ROI difference for B2B fleet buyers?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">GaN car chargers achieve <strong>93-95% efficiency vs 83-85% for silicon</strong>, critical in a 12V vehicle where every watt of waste heat raises internal temperature. GaN V stabilizes at <strong>58.3°C under 100% load vs 82.7°C for silicon</strong> at 25°C ambient. When cabin temps exceed 60°C, GaN V maintains full power output while silicon throttles by 30-50%. For B2B buyers: GaN enables 40% smaller form factor, 30% better heat dissipation, <strong>5+ year lifespan vs 2-3 years for silicon</strong>, and 40-60% higher retail margins on a 15-25% BOM premium. For fleet operators, GaN means chargers that survive summer dashboards and deliver consistent performance across all vehicles.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Do PD 3.1 car chargers support full-speed laptop charging for mobile workforces?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer"><strong>Yes, PD 3.1 car chargers deliver up to 140W</strong> (28V/5A), capable of fast-charging MacBook Pro 16-inch, Dell XPS, and ThinkPad laptops at full speed while driving. A 65W dual-port GaN unit charges a laptop at 45W and a phone at 20W simultaneously. For fleet operators, this eliminates the need for separate laptop power banks and keeps field teams productive between client visits. The <strong>EU Common Charger Directive mandates USB-C for all laptops from April 2026</strong>, making PD 3.1 car chargers a regulatory requirement for European fleet procurement, not just a differentiator.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Retractable vs fixed cable, which costs less over a fleet vehicle's service life?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">Retractable cable car chargers <strong>reduce fleet maintenance costs by 40-60%</strong> over the vehicle service life. Cables last <strong>3-5× longer</strong> vs fixed-cable designs because they're protected inside the housing when not in use, eliminating dashboard heat damage and repeated bending at the strain relief. For ride-share and delivery fleets (500+ vehicles), this translates to <strong>$3-8/vehicle/year in replacement savings</strong>. For OEM orders, retractable mechanisms add $1-2/unit to BOM but support retail premiums of $5-10/unit. Traditional fixed cable: cheaper upfront, 12-18 month typical cable lifespan in fleet use, easier to customize cable length for specific vehicle interiors.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">12V vs 24V, do fleet buyers need different chargers for cars and trucks?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer"><strong>Wide-input (12-24V) GaN chargers eliminate the need for separate SKUs.</strong> Passenger cars and SUVs use 12V DC systems (max 180W from cigarette lighter); trucks, buses, and heavy equipment use 24V DC (max 360W). A wide-input charger automatically detects voltage and adjusts, one SKU covers your entire mixed fleet. This reduces procurement complexity by 50% and eliminates voltage-mismatch returns. The $0.50-1.00 BOM adder for wide-input regulation is 10× cheaper than managing dual-SKU inventory and processing mismatch returns. <strong>Always specify wide-input compatibility in your OEM RFQ</strong> unless you operate a 100% single-vehicle-class fleet.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What certifications are mandatory for car chargers sold in EU and US markets?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer"><strong>EU mandatory:</strong> CE marking, RoHS, and <strong>E-Mark (ECE R10)</strong>, the automotive-specific electromagnetic compatibility certification required for any electronic device used in vehicles. <strong>US mandatory:</strong> FCC Part 15B. <strong>Both markets:</strong> 10-layer circuit protection (over-current, over-voltage, over-temperature, short-circuit, reverse-polarity, over-power, under-voltage lockout, ESD, surge, thermal shutdown). For fleet procurement, always request original certificates (not copies) and verify E-Mark on the issuing body's database. WOWOHCOOL car chargers include CE, FCC, RoHS, and E-Mark as standard. <strong>EU Common Charger Directive 2022/2380 mandates USB-C from December 2024</strong>, car chargers with USB-A only are now non-compliant for EU procurement.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What MOQ and FOB pricing apply to OEM car chargers by wattage tier?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer"><strong>Standard ODM with custom branding:</strong> MOQ 500 units per SKU. <strong>Custom OEM with new tooling:</strong> MOQ 1,000-2,000 units. FOB Shenzhen pricing at 1,000 units: 20W single-port USB-C <strong>$2-4/unit</strong>, 30W dual-port PD+PPS $3-6/unit, 45-65W PD 3.0 dual-port $5-8/unit, 4+ port hub 60W retractable $6-12/unit, 65W GaN dual-port fleet-grade with E-Mark <strong>$8-14/unit</strong>, 24V commercial wide-input $6-10/unit. Retractable cable adds $1-2/unit. Custom branding (laser/silk screen) adds $0.30-0.80/unit. E-Mark certification included at no extra charge from certified factories. Lead time: 25-35 days after sample approval. Payment: 30% deposit, 70% before shipment.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">How do OEM buyers source factory-direct car chargers from verified Shenzhen manufacturers?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer"><strong>Step 1:</strong> Define specs, wattage, port config, cable type (retractable/fixed), 12V or 24V compatibility, target certifications, and branding requirements. <strong>Step 2:</strong> Request quotes from 3+ ISO 9001-certified Shenzhen factories, compare GaN chip generation, MOQ, FOB pricing, E-Mark certification status, and lead times. <strong>Step 3:</strong> Request a same-day video tour of the SMT production line, a real factory complies immediately. <strong>Step 4:</strong> Order 3-5 production-grade samples (3-7 day turnaround), test in actual vehicles at full load in high-temperature conditions. <strong>Step 5:</strong> Verify certifications on official databases. <strong>WOWOHCOOL offers factory-direct OEM car chargers with PD 3.1 up to 140W, GaN V, E-Mark certification, and salt-spray testing, all from a 5,000m² ISO 9001 Shenzhen facility. <a href="/contact/" class="text-brandBlue hover:text-brandOrange underline">Request a consultation for current FOB pricing and lead times.</a></strong></p>
 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
